---
// The "How do I…?" index — the course indexed by the question students ask.
//
// The lessons are sequenced for teaching. This page is sequenced for needing
// help: every entry is a real question in a student's own words, pointed at
// the lesson that answers it. The scaffolding badges make the deliberate
// change in support visible, so a student in Unit 3 knows they are
// SUPPOSED to be writing this one themselves.
//
// Entries come from src/data/asks.ts (single source of truth). Everything is
// assembled into strings here because the page is mostly generated, not
// hand-written markup.
import Base from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { ASK_GROUPS } from '../../data/asks';
import { LEVEL_LABEL } from '../../lib/walkthrough';

const LADDER: { level: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4; shape: string; does: string }[] = [
  { level: 1, shape: 'Every line given and explained', does: 'You type it, and each line is annotated before the next arrives. Nothing is withheld.' },
  { level: 2, shape: 'Shape given, some gaps to fill', does: 'The structure is laid out and the API is named; you work out the details.' },
  { level: 3, shape: 'Spec and API only', does: 'The problem is stated as a specification with the functions listed. No code.' },
  { level: 4, shape: 'The problem, and nothing else', does: 'You have built this shape before. Now build it again, from the question alone.' },
];

const ladderHtml = LADDER.map(
  (r) =>
    '<tr><td><span class="ask-badge l' + r.level + '">' + LEVEL_LABEL[r.level] + '</span></td>' +
    '<td>' + r.shape + '</td><td>' + r.does + '</td></tr>'
).join('');

const groupsHtml = ASK_GROUPS.map(
  (g) =>
    '<div class="ask-group">' +
    '<h2>' + g.title + '</h2>' +
    '<p class="ask-group-blurb">' + g.blurb + '</p>' +
    '<ul class="ask-list">' +
    g.asks
      .map(
        (a) =>
          '<li class="ask-item">' +
          '<span class="ask-q">' + a.q + '</span>' +
          '<span class="ask-where">' +
          '<a class="ask-lesson" href="../lessons/' + a.lesson + '.html">' + a.lesson + '</a>' +
          '<span class="ask-badge l' + a.level + '">' + LEVEL_LABEL[a.level] + '</span>' +
          '</span>' +
          '<p class="ask-note">' + a.note + '</p>' +
          '</li>'
      )
      .join('') +
    '</ul>' +
    '</div>'
).join('');
---
<Base title="How do I…? — AP CS Principles" active="docs" root=".." docsHome="index.html" css="style.css"
  description="Every 'how do I do X in Unity' question this course answers, mapped to the lesson that teaches it — indexed the way students actually search.">
<h1>How do I…?</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Lessons are in teaching order. This page is in <strong>asking order</strong>. If you are
    stuck on something and you know what you want to happen but not how to make it happen,
    find your question below — it is probably already written down, in roughly the words you
    would use.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>This is the intended way to look things up.</strong> Searching a question like
    &ldquo;how do I make an object disappear in Unity&rdquo; is exactly what a working
    programmer does. The difference is that the answers here are written for the version of
    Unity this course uses, and they build on code you have already written.
</p>

<h2>The two questions every lesson asks</h2>

<p>
    Near the top of every lesson in this course, before any code, there is the same block. It
    asks two questions. They never change, and they are the most valuable thing on this site —
    more valuable than any Unity API, because they are what you will still be doing in ten years
    in a language that does not exist yet.
</p>

<ol class="two-questions">
    <li>
        <strong>What are the parts?</strong> A whole feature is too big to hold in your head at
        once. &ldquo;Move the player&rdquo; is not one problem — it is <em>read the input</em>,
        <em>build a direction</em>, <em>apply the movement</em>. &ldquo;Collect a coin&rdquo; is
        not one problem either — it is <em>confirm the thing that touched me was a coin</em>,
        <em>change the count</em>, <em>destroy the object</em>. Almost every student who decides
        they are bad at programming has actually just met a problem they never split up.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Where do I read about each part?</strong> A part you can name is a part you can
        search for. &ldquo;Make the turret shoot&rdquo; returns nothing useful. &ldquo;Clone a
        prefab at a position&rdquo; returns exactly what you need. Every part in the block names
        where to read about it — and a part with no reading attached is not a part yet, it is the
        whole problem wearing a shorter name.
    </li>
</ol>

<p>
    There used to be a third question here — <em>&ldquo;how is this like something I have
    already built?&rdquo;</em> It is still worth asking, and the course is still sequenced so
    that a spike is a coin with consequences and a projectile is a coin with a direction. But
    asked in all 81 lessons it turned into a formality, answered without thinking, and it cost
    more writing than it was worth. Now it is asked only where the connection is the thing that
    makes the problem make sense.
</p>

<p>
    Then it asks you to write the answers down, in the same answer boxes as every other question
    in the course. That is deliberate: the thinking is the assignment, not a warm-up before it.
</p>

<div class="callout info">
    <strong>The block above your lesson is the outline of your solution.</strong>
    If you are staring at a blank script not knowing where to start, scroll up and reread it.
    You are not supposed to hold the whole thing in your head, and nobody who does this for a
    living does either.
</div>

<h2>The support you get changes on purpose</h2>

<p>
    You may have noticed that some lessons hand you almost everything and later ones hand you
    almost nothing. That is deliberate, and it is worth understanding why — because
    <em>&ldquo;my teacher stopped giving me code&rdquo;</em> is the moment most people
    learning to program give up, and it is not supposed to be how it feels.
</p>

<p>
    The rule is: <strong>the first time a concept appears, you get everything explained. Every
    time after that, you get less</strong> — because what you are being asked to do is
    something you have already done once, wearing different clothes. Spikes are a lot like
    coins. A projectile is a lot like a coin with a direction. By the time you build the
    projectile you have already written everything it needs, and you do it alone because you
    <em>can</em>.
</p>

<table class="ladder">
    <tr><th>Badge</th><th>What the lesson gives you</th><th>What you do</th></tr>
    <Fragment set:html={ladderHtml} />
</table>

<p>
    If a lesson feels too hard for where you are, that is real information — it usually means
    an earlier dot did not get connected, not that you are bad at this. Go back to the lesson
    the badge points at and reread the walkthrough. The whole point of writing every line down
    the first time is that you can always go back and find it.
</p>

<div class="callout info">
    <strong>Where the course is right now.</strong> The badges below show what each lesson is
    <em>designed</em> to give you, and the ladder is being built out lesson by lesson as the year
    reaches each one. The badge printed on the lesson page itself is always the truth for that
    lesson: if a lesson still hands you the questions-only version while this page says it gives
    you a walkthrough, ask your teacher rather than assuming you missed something.
    <br><br>
    The rule behind the badges: <strong>the first time a concept appears you get every line
    explained</strong>, and every time it comes back you get less. That is not the course getting
    harder to be unkind. It is the difference between being shown a thing and being able to do it.
</div>

<Fragment set:html={groupsHtml} />

<div class="resources">
    <h3>If your question is not here</h3>
    <p>
        Two other places are worth trying before you ask a human. The
        <a href="reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> lists every
        API this course uses on a single page, which is what you want when you know the name of
        the thing and need its exact spelling. The <a href="index.html">documentation hub</a>
        groups the longer explanations by unit.
    </p>
    <p>
        And if you find the answer to a question that is <em>not</em> on this page — tell your
        teacher. Every entry here started as somebody being stuck.
    </p>
</div>
</Base>
